{:edb/db-before-t 915 :edb/db-after-t 916 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534228 50 #inst "2026-10-11T17:59:59.815Z" 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186151842 1071 "If $ka \\equiv kb \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\,7)$\nis equivalent to $%\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\na \\equiv b \\:(\\textrm{mod} \\,7),$\nwhich of the following could be the value of $k?$\n\n- $k=-12$\n\n- $k=4$\n\n- $k=-14$" 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186151842 1071 "If $ka \\equiv kb \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\,7)$\nis equivalent to $%\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad a \\equiv b \\:(\\textrm{mod} \\,7)$,\nwhich of the following could be the value of $k$?\n\n1. $k=-12$\n2. $k=4$\n3. $k=-14$" 13194139534228 17592186045417 false] [17592186151842 1074 "The division property of modular arithmetic states that\n\n$$\nka\\equiv kb\\quad(\\textrm{mod} \\,n) \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow\\qquad a \\equiv b \\quad(\\textrm{mod}\\, n)\n$$\n\nprovided that $k$ and $n$ are coprime. In other words, we require\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(k,n)=1\n$$\n\nIn this case, the modulus $n=7$. Let's consider each value of $k$ in turn.\n\n- If $k=-12$, then $\\text{gcd}(k,n) = \\text{gcd}(-12,7) = 1$. Therefore, our equivalence is valid for $k=-12$.\n- If $k=4$, then $\\text{gcd}(k,n) = \\text{gcd}(4,7) = 1$. Therefore, our equivalence is valid for $k=4$.\n- If $k=-14$, then $\\text{gcd}(k,n) = \\text{gcd}(-14,7) = 7\\neq 1$. Therefore, our equivalence is *not* valid for $k=-14$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." 13194139534228 17592186045417 false] [17592186151842 1074 "The division property of modular arithmetic states that\n\n$$\nka\\equiv kb\\quad(\\textrm{mod} \\,n) \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow\\qquad a \\equiv b \\quad(\\textrm{mod}\\, n)\n$$\n\nprovided that $k$ and $n$ are coprime. In other words, we require $\\text{gcd}(k,n)=1.$\n\nIn this case, the modulus $n=7.$ Let's consider each value of $k$ in turn.\n\n- If $k=-12,$ then $\\text{gcd}(k,n) = \\text{gcd}(-12,7) = 1.$ Therefore, our equivalence is valid for $k=-12.$\n\n- If $k=4,$ then $\\text{gcd}(k,n) = \\text{gcd}(4,7) = 1.$ Therefore, our equivalence is valid for $k=4.$\n\n- If $k=-14,$ then $\\text{gcd}(k,n) = \\text{gcd}(-14,7) = 7\\neq 1.$ Therefore, our equivalence is *not* valid for $k=-14.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186151843 1071 "Given that $4a \\equiv 4b \\,(\\textrm{mod}\\,8)$, which of the following statements **must** be true?\n\n1. $a \\equiv b \\quad(\\textrm{mod}\\,4)$\n2. $a \\equiv b \\quad(\\textrm{mod}\\,2)$\n3. $a \\equiv b \\quad(\\textrm{mod}\\,8)$" 13194139534228 17592186045417 false] [17592186151843 1071 "Given that $4a \\equiv 4b \\,(\\textrm{mod}\\,8),$ which of the following statements **must** be true?\n\n- $a \\equiv b \\quad(\\textrm{mod}\\,4)$\n\n- $a \\equiv b \\quad(\\textrm{mod}\\,2)$\n\n- $a \\equiv b \\quad(\\textrm{mod}\\,8)$" 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186151843 1074 "The division property of modular arithmetic states that for\n\n$$\nk\\neq 0\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\nka\\equiv kb\\quad(\\textrm{mod} \\,kn) \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow\\qquad a \\equiv b \\quad(\\textrm{mod}\\, n)\n$$\n\nWe're asked to consider the following statement:\n\n$$\n4a \\equiv 4b \\quad(\\textrm{mod}\\,8)\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(4,8) =4\n$$\n\nApplying the division property, we have the following:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}4a & ≡4b & (mod8) \\\\ 4 \\cdot a & ≡4 \\cdot b & (mod4 \\cdot 2) \\\\ 4 \\cdot a & ≡4 \\cdot b & (mod4 \\cdot 2) \\\\ a & ≡b & (mod2)\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." 13194139534228 17592186045417 false] [17592186151843 1074 "The division property of modular arithmetic states that for $k\\neq 0,$ we have\n\n$$\nka\\equiv kb\\quad(\\textrm{mod} \\,kn) \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow\\qquad a \\equiv b \\quad(\\textrm{mod}\\, n).\n$$\n\nWe're asked to consider the following statement:\n\n$$\n4a \\equiv 4b \\quad(\\textrm{mod}\\,8)\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\text{gcd}(4,8) =4.$\n\nApplying the division property, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4a & ≡4b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\ 4\\cdot a & ≡4\\cdot b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,4\\cdot 2) \\\\ \\cancel{4}\\cdot a & ≡\\cancel{4}\\cdot b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,\\cancel{4}\\cdot 2) \\\\ a & ≡b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186151858 1071 "Given that $15a \\equiv 15b \\,(\\textrm{mod}\\,20)$, which of the following statements **must** be true?\n\n1. $a \\equiv b \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\, 20)$\n2. $a \\equiv b \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,4)$\n3. $7a \\equiv 7b \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,4)$" 13194139534228 17592186045417 false] [17592186151858 1071 "Given that $15a \\equiv 15b \\,(\\textrm{mod}\\,20),$ which of the following statements **must** be true?\n\n- $a \\equiv b \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\, 20)$\n\n- $a \\equiv b \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,4)$\n\n- $7a \\equiv 7b \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,4)$" 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186151858 1074 "The division properties of modular arithmetic state the following:\n\n- For $k\\neq 0$, we have\n$ka \\equiv kb \\quad(\\text{mod}\\,kn) \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad a \\equiv b \\quad(\\textrm{mod}\\,n)$.\n- For $\\text{gcd}(k, n) = 1$, we have\n$ka \\equiv kb \\quad(\\text{mod}\\,n) \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad a \\equiv b \\quad(\\textrm{mod}\\,n)$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. Applying the first property, we have the following:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}15a & ≡15b & (mod20) \\\\ 5 \\cdot 3 \\cdot a & ≡5 \\cdot 3 \\cdot b & (mod5 \\cdot 4) \\\\ 5 \\cdot 3 \\cdot a & ≡5 \\cdot 3 \\cdot b & (mod5 \\cdot 4) \\\\ 3a & ≡3b & (mod4)\\end{bmatrix}$\nSince $\\gcd(3,4)=1$, by the second property, we have:\n$3a \\equiv 3b \\quad(\\textrm{mod}\\,4) \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad a \\equiv b \\quad(\\textrm{mod}\\,4)$\n- Statement III is true. By statement II and the second property, we have:\n$a≡b(mod4)⟺7a≡7b(mod4)$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139534228 17592186045417 false] [17592186151858 1074 "The division properties of modular arithmetic state the following:\n\n- For $k\\neq 0,$ we have\n\n$$\nka \\equiv kb \\quad(\\text{mod}\\,kn) \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad a \\equiv b \\quad(\\textrm{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\n- For $\\text{gcd}(k, n) = 1,$ we have \n\n$$\nka \\equiv kb \\quad(\\text{mod}\\,n) \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad a \\equiv b \\quad(\\textrm{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. Applying the first property, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15a & ≡15b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,20) \\\\ 5\\cdot 3\\cdot a & ≡5\\cdot 3\\cdot b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5\\cdot 4) \\\\ \\cancel{5}\\cdot 3\\cdot a & ≡\\cancel{5}\\cdot 3\\cdot b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,\\cancel{5}\\cdot 4) \\\\ 3a & ≡3b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\gcd(3,4)=1,$ by the second property, we have:\n\n$$\n3a \\equiv 3b \\quad(\\textrm{mod}\\,4) \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad a \\equiv b \\quad(\\textrm{mod}\\,4)\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. By statement II and the second property, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4)\\,⟺\\,7a≡7b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186152094 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415043 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186152094 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415047 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186152094 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415048 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186152094 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415051 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186152094 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415052 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186152095 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415042 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186152095 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415050 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186152095 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415053 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186152096 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415041 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186152096 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415044 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186152096 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415045 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186152096 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415046 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186152096 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415049 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186152342 1050 420.0 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186152477 1068 #edb/ref 17592186149414 13194139534228 17592186045417 false] [17592186152477 1068 #edb/ref 17592186149420 13194139534228 17592186045417 false] [17592186152477 1068 #edb/ref 17592186149423 13194139534228 17592186045417 false] [17592186152477 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415018 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186152477 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415020 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186152477 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415024 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186152477 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186152477 1071 "Consider the following congruence:\n\n$$\n5\\cdot 2a≡5\\cdot 2b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,18)\n$$" 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186152477 1071 "Consider the following congruence:\n\n$5 \\cdot 2a≡5 \\cdot 2b(mod18)$\n\nSince $5$ and {{field-1}} are {{field-2}}, this congruence {{field-3}} $2a≡2b(mod18)$." 13194139534228 17592186045417 false] [17592186152477 1074 "The division property of modular arithmetic states that\n\n$$\nka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nprovided that $k$ and $n$ are coprime. In other words, we require $\\text{gcd}(k,n)=1.$\n\nIn our case, the factor $k=5$ and the modulus $n=18$ are coprime since $\\text{gcd}(5,18)=1.$ As a result, we can cancel out $5$ from both sides of the congruence:\n\n$$\n\\cancel{5}\\cdot 2a≡\\cancel{5}\\cdot 2b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,18)\\,⟺\\,2a≡2b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,18)\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following: \n\nSince $5$ and $18$ are $\\text{coprime},$ this congruence $\\text{is equivalent to}$ $2a≡2b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,18).$" 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186152478 1068 #edb/ref 17592186149426 13194139534228 17592186045417 false] [17592186152478 1068 #edb/ref 17592186149432 13194139534228 17592186045417 false] [17592186152478 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415027 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186152478 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415039 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186152478 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186152478 1071 "Consider the following congruence:\n\n$$\n2\\cdot 3a≡2\\cdot 3b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,16)\n$$" 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186152478 1071 "Consider the following congruence:\n\n$2 \\cdot 3a≡2 \\cdot 3b(mod16)$\n\nSince $2$ and $16$ are {{field-1}}, this congruence {{field-2}} $3a≡3b(mod16)$." 13194139534228 17592186045417 false] [17592186152478 1074 "The division property of modular arithmetic states that\n\n$$\nka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nprovided that $k$ and $n$ are coprime. In other words, we require $\\text{gcd}(k,n)=1.$\n\nIn our case, the factor $k=2$ and the modulus $n=16$ are not coprime since $\\text{gcd}(2,16)=2.$ As a result, we cannot cancel out $2$ from both sides of the congruence.\n\nTherefore, we have the following: \n\nSince $2$ and $16$ are $\\text{not coprime},$ this congruence $\\text{is not equivalent to}$ $3a≡3b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,16).$" 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186153452 1064 "In a previous lesson, we saw that congruences obey another scalar multiplication property:\n\n$$\na\\equiv b  \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n) \\quad \\Longrightarrow\\quad ka \\equiv kb \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nThe (second) **division property** of modular arithmetic tells us that\n\n$$\nka\\equiv kb\\quad(\\textrm{mod} \\,n) \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow\\qquad a \\equiv b \\quad(\\textrm{mod}\\, n)\n$$\n\n*provided that $k$ and $n$ are coprime*. In other words, we require $\\textrm{gcd}(k,n)=1.$ \n\n**Caution:** The condition that $k$ and $n$ are coprime is *very* important! The division property does *not* work when $k$ and $n$ are not coprime!\n\nThis might seem a little puzzling, so let's look at some concrete examples:\n\n- Consider the following true statement:\n\n$$\n12 \\equiv 2 \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:5)\n$$\n\nNotice that the two numbers in our congruence have a common factor of ${\\color{blue}2}{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}2} \\cdot 6 \\equiv {\\color{blue}2} \\cdot 1 \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:5)\n$$\n\nSince $\\textrm{gcd}({\\color{blue}2},5)=1,$ we can cancel out ${\\color{blue}2}$ from both sides, keeping the modulus unchanged.\n\n$$\n6 \\equiv 1 \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:5)\n$$\n\n- Next, consider the following true statement:\n\n$$\n12 \\equiv 6 \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:6)\n$$\n\nNotice that the two numbers in our congruence, *and the modulus*, have a common factor of ${\\color{red}3}{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}3} \\cdot 4 \\equiv {\\color{red}3} \\cdot 2  \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:6)\n$$\n\nBecause $\\textrm{gcd}({\\color{red}3},6) = 3 \\neq 1,$ we *cannot* cancel out ${\\color{red}3}$ from both sides. If we do, we get the following *false* statement!\n\n$$\n4\\equiv 2 \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:6).\n$$\n\nIn such cases, we should use the first division property to simplify the congruence." 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186153452 1064 "In a previous lesson, we saw that congruences obey another scalar multiplication property:\n\n$$\na\\equiv b \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n) \\quad \\Longrightarrow\\quad ka \\equiv kb \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nThe (second) **division property** of modular arithmetic tells us that\n\n$$\nka\\equiv kb\\quad(\\textrm{mod} \\,n) \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow\\qquad a \\equiv b \\quad(\\textrm{mod}\\, n)\n$$\n\n*provided that $k$ and $n$ are coprime*. In other words, we require\n\n$$\n\\textrm{gcd}(k,n)=1\n$$\n\n**Caution:** The condition that $k$ and $n$ are coprime is *very* important! The division property does *not* work when $k$ and $n$ are not coprime!\n\nThis might seem a little puzzling, so let's look at some concrete examples:\n\n- Consider the following true statement:\n$12 \\equiv 2 \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:5)$\nNotice that the two numbers in our congruence have a common factor of ${\\color{blue}2}{:}$\n${\\color{blue}2} \\cdot 6 \\equiv {\\color{blue}2} \\cdot 1 \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:5)$\nSince $\\textrm{gcd}({\\color{blue}2},5)=1$, we can cancel out ${\\color{blue}2}$ from both sides, keeping the modulus unchanged.\n$6 \\equiv 1 \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:5)$\n- Next, consider the following true statement:\n$12 \\equiv 6 \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:6)$\nNotice that the two numbers in our congruence, *and the modulus*, have a common factor of ${\\color{red}3}{:}$\n${\\color{red}3} \\cdot 4 \\equiv {\\color{red}3} \\cdot 2 \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:6)$\nBecause $\\textrm{gcd}({\\color{red}3},6) = 3 \\neq 1$, we *cannot* cancel out ${\\color{red}3}$ from both sides. If we do, we get the following *false* statement!\n$4\\equiv 2 \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:6)$.\nIn such cases, we should use the first division property to simplify the congruence." 13194139534228 17592186045417 false] [17592186153453 1064 "In a previous lesson, we saw that congruences obey the following scalar multiplication property:\n\n$$\na\\equiv b  \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n) \\quad \\Longrightarrow\\quad ka \\equiv kb \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,kn)\n$$\n\nIt turns out that for $k\\neq 0,$ we can perform this operation in reverse. That is,\n\n$$\nka \\equiv kb \\quad(\\text{mod}\\,kn) \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad a \\equiv b \\quad(\\textrm{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nTherefore, for $k\\neq 0,$ we have the following **division property,** which we can write as an equivalence:\n\n$$\nka \\equiv kb \\quad(\\text{mod}\\,kn) \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad a \\equiv b \\quad(\\textrm{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the following true statement:\n\n$$\n12 \\equiv 6 \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:6)\n$$\n\nNote that all three numbers in our congruence have a common factor of ${\\color{blue}3}.$\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}3} \\cdot 4 \\equiv {\\color{blue}3} \\cdot 2 \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:{\\color{blue}3} \\cdot 2)\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the division property, we can cancel a factor of ${\\color{blue}3}$ from both sides and the modulus.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\cancel{3}\\cdot 4≡\\cancel{3}\\cdot 2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,\\cancel{3}\\cdot 2) \\\\ 4≡2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt's important to note that the division property does not hold if $k=0.$ For example, consider the following counterexample.\n\n$$\n0\\cdot 2 \\equiv 0\\cdot 1 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow\\qquad 2\\equiv 1 \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,2)\n$$\n\nThe antecedent is true, the consequent is false, and hence the implication is *false*." 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186153453 1064 "In a previous lesson, we saw that congruences obey the following scalar multiplication property:\n\n$$\na\\equiv b \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n) \\quad \\Longrightarrow\\quad ka \\equiv kb \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,kn)\n$$\n\nIt turns out that for\n\n$$\nk\\neq 0\n$$\n\nwe can perform this operation in reverse. That is,\n\n$$\nka \\equiv kb \\quad(\\text{mod}\\,kn) \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad a \\equiv b \\quad(\\textrm{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nTherefore, for\n\n$$\nk\\neq 0\n$$\n\nwe have the following **division property,** which we can write as an equivalence:\n\n$$\nka \\equiv kb \\quad(\\text{mod}\\,kn) \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad a \\equiv b \\quad(\\textrm{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the following true statement:\n\n$$\n12 \\equiv 6 \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:6)\n$$\n\nNote that all three numbers in our congruence have a common factor of ${\\color{blue}3}$.\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}3} \\cdot 4 \\equiv {\\color{blue}3} \\cdot 2 \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:{\\color{blue}3} \\cdot 2)\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the division property, we can cancel a factor of ${\\color{blue}3}$ from both sides and the modulus.\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\cdot 4≡3 \\cdot 2 & (mod3 \\cdot 2) \\\\ 4≡2 & (mod2)\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nIt's important to note that the division property does not hold if $k=0$. For example, consider the following counterexample.\n\n$$\n0\\cdot 2 \\equiv 0\\cdot 1 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow\\qquad 2\\equiv 1 \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,2)\n$$\n\nThe antecedent is true, the consequent is false, and hence the implication is *false*." 13194139534228 17592186045417 false] [17592186153454 1064 "Let's see why the division properties work.\n\n- The proof of the first property is straightforward:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,kn) & \\,⟺\\,kn\\,|\\,(ka-kb) \\\\  & \\,⟺\\,kn\\,|\\,k(a-b) \\\\  & \\,⟺\\,\\cancel{k}n\\,|\\,\\cancel{k}(a-b) \\\\  & \\,⟺\\,n\\,|\\,(a-b) \\\\  & \\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To prove the second division property, suppose that $\\textrm{gcd}(k,n)=1$ and ${\\color{blue}k}a\\equiv {\\color{blue}k}b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\,n).$ Then, according to the definition of congruent integers, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n) & \\,⟺\\,n\\,|\\,(ka-kb) \\\\  & \\,⟺\\,n\\,|\\,k(a-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $k$ and $n$ are coprime and $k(a - b)$ is divisible by $n,$ we have that $(a - b)$ must be divisible by $n.$ Now, using the definition again, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n\\,|\\,(a-b) & \\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186153454 1064 "Let's see why the division properties work.\n\n- The proof of the first property is straightforward:\n$ka≡kb(modkn) \\mid ⟺kn\\begin{vmatrix}(ka - kb) \\\\ ⟺kn\\end{vmatrix}k(a - b); ⟺kn\\begin{vmatrix}k(a - b) \\\\ ⟺n\\end{vmatrix}(a - b); ⟺a≡b(modn)$\n\n- To prove the second division property, suppose that $\\textrm{gcd}(k,n)=1$ and ${\\color{blue}k}a\\equiv {\\color{blue}k}b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\,n)$. Then, according to the definition of congruent integers, we obtain\n$ka≡kb(modn) \\mid ⟺n\\begin{vmatrix}(ka - kb) \\\\ ⟺n\\end{vmatrix}k(a - b)$.\nSince $k$ and $n$ are coprime and $k(a - b)$ is divisible by $n$, we have that $(a - b)$ must be divisible by $n$. Now, using the definition again, we get\n$n \\mid (a - b) \\mid ⟺a≡b(modn)$." 13194139534228 17592186045417 false] [17592186414929 1080 "coprime" 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186414929 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186414929 1083 #uuid "06f2ac01-f5af-5965-86e1-c7f62856aeee" 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186414930 1080 "II only" 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186414930 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186414930 1083 #uuid "09d37251-cd85-5560-a010-3e2285781892" 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186414931 1080 "I and II only" 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186414931 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186414931 1083 #uuid "0ab1a91d-d33a-5e51-b220-e0e4da05d420" 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186414932 1080 "is not equivalent to" 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186414932 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186414932 1083 #uuid "0d2b6db7-48a7-5c5f-8a77-2fe48526a608" 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186414933 1080 "I and II only" 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186414933 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186414933 1083 #uuid "0d32cab0-a947-5df3-b52a-1e20d4a89706" 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186414934 1080 "not coprime" 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186414934 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186414934 1083 #uuid "0d52c292-fdaf-50b0-9c28-3b056bd0cb36" 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186414935 1080 "III only" 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186414935 1082 #edb/ref 1759218