)$\n- $4a≡4b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,12)$" 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186415041 1072 #uuid "00e9a03d-cb25-5bd2-bcfe-67d16e1b93d4" 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186415041 1074 "The division properties of modular arithmetic state the following:\n\n- For $k\\ne 0,$ we have\n\n$$\nka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,kn)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\n- For $\\text{gcd}(k,n)=1,$ we have \n\n$$\nka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. Applying the first property, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5a & ≡5b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,15) \\\\ 5\\cdot a & ≡5\\cdot b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5\\cdot 3) \\\\ \\cancel{5}\\cdot a & ≡\\cancel{5}\\cdot b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,\\cancel{5}\\cdot 3) \\\\ a & ≡b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. By statement II and the first property, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)\\,⟺\\,4a≡4b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,12)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\n.\"" 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186415042 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415025 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186415042 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415038 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186415042 1069 "q-213286" 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186415042 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186415042 1071 "Consider the following congruence:\n\n$$\n5\\cdot 2a≡5\\cdot 2b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,10)\n$$" 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186415042 1072 #uuid "0db74dcd-d670-5d6c-b7fa-69c8b873b4a0" 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186415042 1074 "The division property of modular arithmetic states that\n\n$$\nka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nprovided that $k$ and $n$ are coprime. In other words, we require $\\text{gcd}(k,n)=1.$\n\nIn our case, the factor $k=5$ and the modulus $n=10$ are not coprime since $\\text{gcd}(5,10)=5.$ As a result, we cannot cancel out $5$ from both sides of the congruence.\n\nTherefore, we have the following: \n\nSince $5$ and $10$ are $\\text{not coprime},$ this congruence $\\text{is not equivalent to}$ $2a≡2b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,10).$" 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186415043 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415034 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186415043 1069 "q-103191" 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186415043 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186415043 1071 "Given that $6a≡6b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,9),$ which of the following statements **must** be true?\n\n- $2a≡2b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)$\n- $3a≡3b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6)$\n- $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,9)$" 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186415043 1072 #uuid "0ff193af-a6fe-56c6-94b7-27cf32c1ffe5" 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186415043 1074 "The division property of modular arithmetic states that for $k\\ne 0,$ we have\n\n$$\nka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,kn)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nWe're asked to consider the following statement:\n\n$$\n6a≡6b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,9)\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\text{gcd}(6,9)=3.$\n\nApplying the division property, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6a & ≡6b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\ 3\\cdot 2\\cdot a & ≡3\\cdot 2\\cdot b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,3\\cdot 3) \\\\ \\cancel{3}\\cdot 2\\cdot a & ≡\\cancel{3}\\cdot 2\\cdot b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,\\cancel{3}\\cdot 3) \\\\ 2a & ≡2b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186415044 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415036 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186415044 1069 "q-77149" 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186415044 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186415044 1071 "Given that $6a≡6b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8),$ which of the following statements **must** be true?\n\n- $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4)$\n- $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8)$\n- $4a≡4b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,16)$" 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186415044 1072 #uuid "3f8540b5-4c77-553b-a7f5-1fd3c7ffd53b" 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186415044 1074 "The division properties of modular arithmetic state the following:\n\n- For $k\\ne 0,$ we have\n\n$$\nka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,kn)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\n- For $\\text{gcd}(k,n)=1,$ we have \n\n$$\nka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. Applying the first property, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6a & ≡6b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\ 2\\cdot 3\\cdot a & ≡2\\cdot 3\\cdot b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,2\\cdot 4) \\\\ \\cancel{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot a & ≡\\cancel{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,\\cancel{2}\\cdot 4) \\\\ 3a & ≡3b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $gcd(3,4)=1,$ by the second property, we have:\n\n$$\n3a≡3b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4)\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. By statement I and the first property, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4)\\,⟺\\,4a≡4b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,16)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186415045 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415035 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186415045 1069 "q-213296" 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186415045 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186415045 1071 "Given that $10a≡10b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6),$ which of the following statements **must** be true?\n\n- $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)$\n- $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6)$\n- $5a≡5b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,15)$" 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186415045 1072 #uuid "73a43b1a-0275-523e-ad37-d72b83b2b870" 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186415045 1074 "The division properties of modular arithmetic state the following:\n\n- For $k\\ne 0,$ we have\n\n$$\nka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,kn)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\n- For $\\text{gcd}(k,n)=1,$ we have \n\n$$\nka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. Applying the first property, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10a & ≡10b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\ 2\\cdot 5\\cdot a & ≡2\\cdot 5\\cdot b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,2\\cdot 3) \\\\ \\cancel{2}\\cdot 5\\cdot a & ≡\\cancel{2}\\cdot 5\\cdot b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,\\cancel{2}\\cdot 3) \\\\ 5a & ≡5b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $gcd(5,3)=1,$ by the second property, we have:\n\n$$\n5a≡5b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. By statement I and the first property, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)\\,⟺\\,5a≡5b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,15)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186415046 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415033 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186415046 1069 "q-213394" 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186415046 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186415046 1071 "Given that $10a≡10b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4),$ which of the following statements **must** be true?\n\n- $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4)$\n- $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2)$\n- $9a≡9b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2)$" 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186415046 1072 #uuid "a253797f-7a56-5114-8318-23c6ded7e919" 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186415046 1074 "The division properties of modular arithmetic state the following:\n\n- For $k\\ne 0,$ we have\n\n$$\nka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,kn)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\n- For $\\text{gcd}(k,n)=1,$ we have \n\n$$\nka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. Applying the first property, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10a & ≡10b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,4) \\\\ 2\\cdot 5\\cdot a & ≡2\\cdot 5\\cdot b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,2\\cdot 2) \\\\ \\cancel{2}\\cdot 5\\cdot a & ≡\\cancel{2}\\cdot 5\\cdot b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,\\cancel{2}\\cdot 5) \\\\ 5a & ≡5b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $gcd(5,2)=1,$ by the second property, we have:\n\n$$\n5a≡5b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2)\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. By statement II and the second property, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2)\\,⟺\\,9a≡9b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186415047 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415040 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186415047 1069 "q-103171" 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186415047 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186415047 1071 "Given that $2a≡2b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8),$ which of the following statements **must** be true?\n\n- $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6)$\n- $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4)$\n- $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8)$" 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186415047 1072 #uuid "c4019e4a-38d9-5f9b-be44-5c0d23af5290" 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186415047 1074 "The division property of modular arithmetic states that for $k\\ne 0,$ we have\n\n$$\nka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,kn)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nWe're asked to consider the following statement:\n\n$$\n2a≡2b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8)\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\text{gcd}(2,8)=2.$\n\nApplying the division property, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2a & ≡2b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\ 2\\cdot a & ≡2\\cdot b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,2\\cdot 4) \\\\ \\cancel{2}\\cdot a & ≡\\cancel{2}\\cdot b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,\\cancel{2}\\cdot 4) \\\\ a & ≡b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186415048 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415023 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186415048 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415030 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186415048 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415031 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186415048 1069 "q-213274" 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186415048 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186415048 1071 "Given that $9a≡9b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,15),$ construct a statement that also must be true:" 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186415048 1072 #uuid "c54d6f9f-c7e1-5ec9-825f-3e37025371fc" 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186415048 1074 "The division property of modular arithmetic states that for $k\\ne 0,$ we have\n\n$$\nka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,kn)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have a common factor of $k=3,$ and the congruence can be written as\n\n$$\n3\\cdot 3a≡3\\cdot 3b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3\\cdot 5).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can cancel out the common factor on both sides of the congruence and the modulus:\n\n$$\n\\cancel{3}\\cdot 3a≡\\cancel{3}\\cdot 3b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,\\cancel{3}\\cdot 5)\\,⟺\\,3a≡3b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,5)\n$$" 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186415049 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415037 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186415049 1069 "q-213392" 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186415049 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186415049 1071 "Given that $3a≡3b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,9),$ which of the following statements **must** be true?\n\n- $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)$\n- $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,9)$\n- $4a≡4b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)$" 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186415049 1072 #uuid "ca808cf3-f807-5d95-a81f-b5460b52df5b" 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186415049 1074 "The division properties of modular arithmetic state the following:\n\n- For $k\\ne 0,$ we have\n\n$$\nka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,kn)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\n- For $\\text{gcd}(k,n)=1,$ we have \n\n$$\nka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. Applying the first property, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3a & ≡3b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\ 3\\cdot a & ≡3\\cdot b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,3\\cdot 3) \\\\ \\cancel{3}\\cdot a & ≡\\cancel{3}\\cdot b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,\\cancel{3}\\cdot 3) \\\\ a & ≡b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. By statement I and the second property, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)\\,⟺\\,4a≡4b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186415050 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415021 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186415050 1069 "q-103026" 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186415050 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186415050 1071 "If \n$ka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8)$\nis equivalent to\n$a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8),$\nwhich of the following could be the value of $k?$\n\n- $k=8$\n- $k=-12$\n- $k=-15$" 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186415050 1072 #uuid "cdd37720-ffc4-552d-8b81-f3aa2f866efb" 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186415050 1074 "The division property of modular arithmetic states that\n\n$$\nka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nprovided that $k$ and $n$ are coprime. In other words, we require $\\text{gcd}(k,n)=1.$\n\nIn this case, the modulus $n=8.$ Let's consider each value of $k$ in turn.\n\n- If $k=8,$ then $\\text{gcd}(k,n)=\\text{gcd}(8,8)=8\\ne 1.$ Therefore, our equivalence is *not* valid for $k=8.$\n\n- If $k=-12,$ then $\\text{gcd}(k,n)=\\text{gcd}(-12,8)=4\\ne 1.$ Therefore, our equivalence is *not* valid for $k=-12.$\n\n- If $k=-15,$ then $\\text{gcd}(k,n)=\\text{gcd}(-15,8)=1.$ Therefore, our equivalence is valid for $k=-15.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186415051 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415026 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186415051 1069 "q-103168" 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186415051 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186415051 1071 "Given that $3a≡3b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,12),$ which of the following statements **must** be true?\n\n- $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)$\n- $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4)$\n- $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6)$" 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186415051 1072 #uuid "d23e3ed0-5575-5880-b9bf-7ff1adfe9936" 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186415051 1074 "The division property of modular arithmetic states that for $k\\ne 0,$ we have\n\n$$\nka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,kn)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nWe're asked to consider the following statement:\n\n$$\n3a≡3b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,12)\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\text{gcd}(3,12)=3.$\n\nApplying the division property, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3a & ≡3b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,12) \\\\ 3\\cdot a & ≡3\\cdot b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,3\\cdot 4) \\\\ \\cancel{3}\\cdot a & ≡\\cancel{3}\\cdot b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,\\cancel{3}\\cdot 4) \\\\ a & ≡b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186415052 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415019 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186415052 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415028 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186415052 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415032 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186415052 1069 "q-213273" 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186415052 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186415052 1071 "Given that $8a≡8b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6),$ construct a statement that also must be true:" 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186415052 1072 #uuid "e3dce9be-488e-597e-98f1-e5e643b35fa5" 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186415052 1074 "The division property of modular arithmetic states that for $k\\ne 0,$ we have\n\n$$\nka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,kn)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have a common factor of $k=2,$ and the congruence can be written as\n\n$$\n2\\cdot 4a≡2\\cdot 4b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2\\cdot 3).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can cancel out the common factor on both sides of the congruence and the modulus:\n\n$$\n\\cancel{2}\\cdot 4a≡\\cancel{2}\\cdot 4b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,\\cancel{2}\\cdot 3)\\,⟺\\,4a≡4b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)\n$$" 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186415053 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415029 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186415053 1069 "q-103018" 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186415053 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186415053 1071 "If \n$ka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,10)$\nis equivalent to\n$a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,10),$\nwhich of the following could be the value of $k?$\n\n- $k=12$\n- $k=3$\n- $k=-5$" 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186415053 1072 #uuid "eaf9f361-8af6-5a67-abf5-86a0bbac7a1d" 13194139534228 17592186045417 true] [17592186415053 1074 "The division property of modular arithmetic states that\n\n$$\nka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nprovided that $k$ and $n$ are coprime. In other words, we require $\\text{gcd}(k,n)=1.$\n\nIn this case, the modulus $n=10.$ Let's consider each value of $k$ in turn.\n\n- If $k=12,$ then $\\text{gcd}(k,n)=\\text{gcd}(12,10)=2\\ne 1.$ Therefore, our equivalence is *not* valid for $k=12.$\n\n- If $k=3,$ then $\\text{gcd}(k,n)=\\text{gcd}(3,10)=1.$ Therefore, our equivalence is valid for $k=3.$\n\n- If $k=-5,$ then $\\text{gcd}(k,n)=\\text{gcd}(-5,10)=5\\ne 1.$ Therefore, our equivalence is *not* valid for $k=-5.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139534228 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-06f2ac01-f5af-5965-86e1-c7f62856aeee" 17592186414929 "answer-09d3725